    len -= 3;
} while (len > 2);
```

This can write up to 3 extra bytes beyond `end` if `len` is large enough and `out` is close to `end`. But wait, the loop condition checks `out < end`, so it should stop before writing past `end`. Unless there's a specific edge case...

